@* User Guide — Getting started: what BirkNext does, the first-time path, core concepts, importing and Recommended Workflow.
   Anchors here are literal: Documentation Health and the guide tests read this file. *@
@using BirkNext.Web.Services
@using static BirkNext.Web.Components.Guide.GuideCallout

<section id="overview" class="ug-section ug-topic" data-topic="overview" aria-labelledby="overview-title">
    <h2 id="overview-title" class="ug-section-title" tabindex="-1">What BirkNext does</h2>
    <GuideArticleMeta Topic="overview" />
    <p class="ug-lead">BirkNext brings together project documents, source analysis, traceability, quality reviews and controlled runtime testing. It shows the evidence it has and the limits of that evidence, and it never treats every feature as relevant to every project.</p>
    <div class="ug-pillars">
        <div class="ug-pillar"><h3>Project &amp; evidence</h3><p>Import packages, bind them to a logical Project, and explore documents, source and generated documentation.</p></div>
        <div class="ug-pillar"><h3>Test setup</h3><p>Configure the Target Environment, connected systems, integrations, Test Data and schemas.</p></div>
        <div class="ug-pillar"><h3>Testing</h3><p>Run safety-gated runtime, integration and performance tests, and read results with their evidence.</p></div>
        <div class="ug-pillar"><h3>Review &amp; governance</h3><p>Record review decisions, follow documentation drift and see what blocks readiness — and who owns the fix.</p></div>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="start-here" class="ug-section ug-topic" data-topic="start-here" aria-labelledby="start-here-title">
    <h2 id="start-here-title" class="ug-section-title" tabindex="-1">Start here</h2>
    <GuideArticleMeta Topic="start-here" />
    <p class="ug-lead">A first-time path. Skip any step your project does not need: no step is required of every project, and one package rarely contains everything.</p>
    <ol class="ug-steps">
        <li><span class="ug-step-title">Import project evidence</span><span class="ug-step-text">One or more ZIPs: source and docs together, or separate packages for docs, contracts or infrastructure. <a href="/project-import">Import Project</a></span></li>
        <li><span class="ug-step-title">Analyze source where it exists</span><span class="ug-step-text">Import creates the Source Analysis snapshot automatically; a docs-only package has none and is still valid.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ug-step-title">Configure the logical Project</span><span class="ug-step-text">Tell the environment which system it tests. <a href="@UserGuideContent.Href("project-model")">Project model</a></span></li>
        <li><span class="ug-step-title">Bind required evidence</span><span class="ug-step-text">Bind each required role (application source, documentation, contracts …) to a package; optional roles never block.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ug-step-title">Configure the Target Environment</span><span class="ug-step-text">URL, authentication and discovery — needed only for runtime testing. <a href="@UserGuideContent.Href("target-environment")">Target Environment</a></span></li>
        <li><span class="ug-step-title">Review Connected Systems</span><span class="ug-step-text">Fix blockers in Needs attention before testing against external systems. <a href="@UserGuideContent.Href("connected-systems")">Connected Systems</a></span></li>
        <li><span class="ug-step-title">Prepare Test Data</span><span class="ug-step-text">Approve identities, values and fixtures that tests reference. <a href="@UserGuideContent.Href("test-data")">Test Data</a></span></li>
        <li><span class="ug-step-title">Follow Recommended Workflow</span><span class="ug-step-text">It names the next action for your actual evidence. <a href="/getting-started">Recommended Workflow</a></span></li>
        <li><span class="ug-step-title">Run the relevant reviews and tests</span><span class="ug-step-text">Only those that apply to your project; Not applicable is never a failure.</span></li>
    </ol>

    <h3 id="tester-package" class="ug-subtitle">Start BirkNext on Windows (Tester Package)</h3>
    <p>Testers use the <strong>Windows Tester Package</strong> (<code>BirkNext-Tester-Windows-x64-&lt;version&gt;.zip</code>) from the approved distribution. It contains everything BirkNext needs: its own local database and k6, so no Docker, Podman, .NET or database installation is needed. Developers who run BirkNext from source use a different setup; testers should not.</p>
    <ol class="ug-steps ug-steps-compact">
        <li><span class="ug-step-title">Extract the whole ZIP to a short folder</span><span class="ug-step-text">For example <code>C:\BirkNext</code>. Use <em>Extract All</em>; do not run BirkNext from inside the ZIP. The package has deep folders: keep the extract folder short (well under 140 characters), or Windows may silently leave files out.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ug-step-title">Start <code>Start-BirkNext.exe</code></span><span class="ug-step-text">A window shows each step. The first start prepares the local database and can take a minute or more; later starts are faster.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ug-step-title">Use the browser that opens</span><span class="ug-step-text">The browser BirkNext opens is connected automatically. Keep the BirkNext window open while you work; closing it (or Ctrl+C) stops BirkNext.</span></li>
    </ol>
    <ul class="ug-list">
        <li><strong>Connection code.</strong> The start window shows a code such as <code>ABCD-EFGH-JKLM</code>. You only need it for a browser BirkNext did not open itself, for a link opened more than two minutes after start, or after BirkNext restarts. A link that opens BirkNext works once; the code can be typed in any browser on this PC.</li>
        <li><strong>Your data</strong> is kept per Windows user in <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\BirkNext\TesterNative</code>, outside the package, so replacing the package with a newer version keeps it. Starting <code>Start-BirkNext.exe</code> again while it runs opens the running BirkNext instead of a second copy.</li>
        <li><strong>If something goes wrong</strong>, the window says what happened and what to do, and stays open until you press Enter. <code>Start-BirkNext.exe --diagnostics</code> creates a diagnostics file without passwords to send to the BirkNext owner. See <a href="@UserGuideContent.Href("troubleshooting")">Troubleshooting</a> for the common start-up problems.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section id="concepts" class="ug-section ug-topic" data-topic="concepts" aria-labelledby="concepts-title">
    <h2 id="concepts-title" class="ug-section-title" tabindex="-1">Core concepts</h2>
    <GuideArticleMeta Topic="concepts" />
    <div class="ug-card-grid">
        <article class="ug-card"><h3>Applicability, coverage and quality</h3><p><strong>Applicable</strong> means a review fits the project; <strong>NotApplicable</strong> (N/A) means it does not. <strong>Unsupported</strong> means the tool cannot assess a relevant technology — a tool limitation, not a project quality problem. <strong>NotTested</strong>, <strong>Unavailable</strong>, <strong>NotAssessed</strong> and <strong>No evidence</strong> describe missing evidence and never mean failure. Quality scores use assessed checks; coverage is reported separately. If nothing was assessed, no score is inferred.</p></article>
        <article class="ug-card"><h3>Evidence maturity</h3><p>Documented → source-confirmed → configured → observed → runtime-verified → executed → passed. A later stage is never implied by an earlier one: a designed test is not an executed test, and execution is not a pass. Source evidence does not prove runtime behavior; a pipeline definition shows intended steps, not a successful run. No automated violation is not full compliance.</p></article>
        <article class="ug-card"><h3>Snapshots and provenance</h3><p>A Source Analysis snapshot is an immutable record with a fingerprint, timestamp and provenance. New analysis creates another snapshot; it never rewrites history. Current, historical, stale, needs refresh and not comparable are different states. A snapshot is not a deployment.</p></article>
        <article class="ug-card"><h3>Analyzers, providers, engines, extensions</h3><p>An analyzer interprets source; a provider connects to or observes a system; an engine executes a test; an extension supplies project-specific behavior. Available does not mean used, and supported does not mean applicable. M01 – Gradert tilgang (Kode 6/7) is the review of graded access provided by the M2LB child-security-classification extension; its classification evidence is shared with other extensions such as Skolenærvær Testing.</p></article>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="getting-started" class="ug-section ug-topic" data-topic="getting-started" aria-labelledby="getting-started-title">
    <h2 id="getting-started-title" class="ug-section-title" tabindex="-1">Import a project</h2>
    <GuideArticleMeta Topic="getting-started" />
    <p class="ug-lead"><a href="/project-import">Import Project</a> takes one ZIP, validates it once and shows what it found before anything is imported. Import succeeds on its own — configuring a Project afterwards is offered, never forced.</p>

    <p><strong>Import ZIP</strong> is always the button at the top right of Import Project, before and after your first import. When an application already exists, choose what the ZIP is: <em>Add evidence to current application</em> (the default: source code or documents for the same application; nothing already imported is replaced) or <em>Import another application</em> (a different application; the current one stays as it is). A new version of the same source is imported the same way and kept beside the earlier one; choose which version is analysed in <a href="@UserGuideContent.Href("source-management")">Source Management</a>. <em>Cancel</em> or <em>Choose another file</em> before Import changes nothing, and a failed import keeps everything imported before.</p>

    <h3 id="project-inputs" class="ug-subtitle">Three kinds of input</h3>
    <ul class="ug-list">
        <li><strong>Project documents</strong> — Specification, Constitution, Plan, Tasks, Data Model, Research. Read by the Explorers, Document Quality Review and Requirements Traceability.</li>
        <li><strong>Source</strong> — becomes a Source Analysis snapshot; no second upload. You can also analyze source once directly in <a href="/source-analysis">Source Analysis</a> for a source-only archive. Feeds Technology Coverage, Dependency Review, Pipeline Review and the source-based reviews.</li>
        <li><strong>Target Environment</strong> — the deployed system; needed only for runtime reviews (frontend, API, integration, performance, Critical E2E).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A document-only project never waits for source or a target, and a source-only project never waits for documents. <a href="/demo-projects">Demo Projects</a> are optional built-in examples that read through the same workspace as an import.</p>

    <h3 id="project-import" class="ug-subtitle">What happens on import</h3>
    <ol class="ug-steps ug-steps-compact">
        <li><span class="ug-step-title">Preview</span><span class="ug-step-text">Documents by artifact role, detected source and technologies. Nothing is imported yet.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ug-step-title">Import</span><span class="ug-step-text">Documents become artifact revisions; source becomes a snapshot. Both share the import identity and archive fingerprint.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ug-step-title">Next</span><span class="ug-step-text">BirkNext may show <em>Project context available</em> (Configure Project / Configure later), <em>Required evidence candidate found</em> (Review &amp; bind) or <em>New compatible project evidence available</em> (Review project evidence).</span></li>
    </ol>
    <GuideCallout Kind="CalloutKind.Important"><p>A documents-only archive (<em>No source detected</em>) and a source-only archive (<em>No supported project artifacts detected</em>) are complete, neutral outcomes. <em>Imported with notes</em> means something is partial — never a failure. Imported is not reviewed, approved or passed.</p></GuideCallout>

    <ReviewDisclosure Text="Advanced details: archive limits, re-import and staging" CollapseText="Hide advanced details" TestId="ug-import-advanced">
        <p>Archives are up to 50 MB with the Source Analysis safety limits: no traversal, absolute or UNC paths, no symbolic links, bounded entry count and expanded size. The previewed archive is staged on the backend for 30 minutes and survives a backend restart; after that the preview expires. Only a genuine source failure offers a retry from the staged archive. A rejected archive activates nothing.</p>
        <p>Importing the same archive again is recognized by its SHA-256 fingerprint: documents are not duplicated and a current snapshot is reused. A different archive creates a new import and a new snapshot; earlier imports and snapshots stay as history. The client file name is metadata only; no local path is stored.</p>
    </ReviewDisclosure>

    <h3 id="multiple-documents" class="ug-subtitle">Several documents for one role, and modules</h3>
    <p>BirkNext keeps <strong>every</strong> detected candidate and never shows Spec-Kit templates as candidates. Detected is not selected: a candidate is used when it is the only one in its scope, or when you choose it. File names, folder numbers and classification confidence never decide.</p>
    <p>A monorepo with several Spec-Kit projects is shown as <em>modules</em>; each keeps its own selection, and choosing for one never changes another. Choose the <em>module to review</em> in any Explorer or under <em>Artifact candidates</em> on Import Project.</p>

    <h3 id="sample-project-discovery" class="ug-subtitle">How documents are found</h3>
    <p>Discovery scans recursively (skipping <code>bin</code>, <code>obj</code>, <code>node_modules</code>, <code>.git</code>) and classifies Markdown by role from front matter, canonical file names and document structure. Canonical names such as <code>spec.md</code> are hints, not requirements. Every role is optional: a missing role shows <em>Not found</em>. Ambiguous documents appear under <em>Needs review</em>; unsupported ones under <em>Other documents</em>.</p>

    <h3 id="current-workspace" class="ug-subtitle">One current workspace</h3>
    <p>Dashboard, Recommended Workflow, the Explorers and the sidebar read the same current workspace. <em>Not saved</em>, <em>Auto-saved</em> and <em>Saved</em> describe saving only. Pages count artifact <em>roles</em>; availability, selection, analysis and review are separate states. A score with nothing assessed shows “—”, not 0%.</p>

    <h3 id="workflows" class="ug-subtitle">Typical paths</h3>
    <ul class="ug-list">
        <li><strong>Document-only:</strong> import documents → Explorers → Requirements Traceability → applicable Document Quality Review packs.</li>
        <li><strong>Source-only:</strong> import the ZIP or analyze source once → Technology Coverage → Dependency and Pipeline reviews.</li>
        <li><strong>Runtime:</strong> configure a Target Environment → Endpoint/Browser Discovery → API or Frontend Quality Review → Critical E2E.</li>
        <li><strong>Integrations:</strong> Connected Systems and Integration Catalog → Integration Quality Review → domain test packs such as Skolenærvær Testing.</li>
        <li><strong>Performance:</strong> engine readiness → scenario, workload, thresholds → readiness → run → results → baseline.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section id="project-quality" class="ug-section ug-topic" data-topic="project-quality" aria-labelledby="project-quality-title">
    <h2 id="project-quality-title" class="ug-section-title" tabindex="-1">Project Quality and the next action</h2>
    <GuideArticleMeta Topic="project-quality" />
    <p class="ug-lead">Two pages, one project context and one recommendation. <a href="/dashboard">Project Quality</a> answers <em>what do we know about this project's evidence, reviews, tests and risks</em>; <a href="/getting-started">Recommended Workflow</a> answers <em>what to do next, why, and what must happen first</em>. Both show the <strong>same next action</strong>, worded the same way, from one workflow engine; this Help Center explains <em>how</em> to do it ("Learn how").</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Imported is not reviewed, reviewed is not tested.</strong> Found documents and modules are evidence that exists; a review decision records a human review; only <strong>Execution evidence</strong> (recorded runs in Test Execution &amp; Evidence for the active Target Environment) says something was executed. Generated tests are not executions, and "no recorded runs" never means healthy.</li>
        <li><strong>Scores are scores.</strong> A document or audit score is shown as a band ("Score 80+"); no project policy turns it into a pass. Readiness derived from documents is labelled as such, and release readiness is owned by Release Readiness.</li>
        <li><strong>Unavailable is not zero.</strong> When evidence cannot be read the card says Unavailable or Not read.</li>
        <li>Recommended Workflow groups the work into five <strong>stages</strong> — understand the project, review requirements &amp; traceability, assess technical quality, prepare &amp; run tests, review evidence &amp; release readiness. A stage moves only with recorded evidence (a decision, a run), never because a page was opened. You can open any module directly; the stages describe, the next action recommends.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section id="system-understanding" class="ug-section ug-topic" data-topic="system-understanding" aria-labelledby="system-understanding-title">
    <h2 id="system-understanding-title" class="ug-section-title" tabindex="-1">System Understanding (the system under test)</h2>
    <GuideArticleMeta Topic="system-understanding" />
    <p class="ug-lead"><a href="/system-understanding">System Understanding</a> explains the <strong>system you are testing</strong> — what it does, how information moves, what happens at each step, which rules apply and what to test. It is not this Help Center (how to use BirkNext), not Integration Topology (what is connected according to evidence) and not Specification Review (what requirements say).</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Overview &amp; flow</strong>: the system in one minute, then the flow. The main journey runs along the top; side flows (for example identity lookup, supporting data, placements, acknowledgments, periodic export) are drawn with their own line style. Select a component or a connection for what goes in and out, the transport, the data format and its evidence. "Flow as text" gives the same information as a list.</li>
        <li><strong>Step by step</strong>: each step says what triggers it, what happens, what can go wrong and how a tester can check it.</li>
        <li><strong>Data &amp; contracts</strong>: what moves between components and under which contract. Versions are listed per source snapshot; a version seen in source does not say what is deployed.</li>
        <li><strong>Business rules</strong> and <strong>What to test</strong>: rules with separate implementation and verification status; test guidance is <em>not</em> test coverage.</li>
        <li><strong>Evidence classes</strong>: <em>In source</em> (code exists — not proof that the integration works), <em>Test defined</em> (a test exists, not executed here), <em>Specified</em>, <em>Inferred</em>, <em>Needs clarification</em>, <em>Conflicting</em>, <em>Not assessed</em>; <em>Test verified</em> and <em>Runtime verified</em> need a current execution.</li>
        <li><strong>Revisions</strong>: a guide is versioned. A curated guide starts as an unreviewed draft. Readers see the published revision; an edit opens a new draft, which is submitted, approved and published. A published revision is never changed. When new source snapshots are imported, the guide says it may need review — it is never rewritten automatically. Editing and approving need the configuration-write permission.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section id="manual-testing" class="ug-section ug-topic" data-topic="manual-testing" aria-labelledby="manual-testing-title">
    <h2 id="manual-testing-title" class="ug-section-title" tabindex="-1">Manual testing</h2>
    <GuideArticleMeta Topic="manual-testing" />
    <p class="ug-lead">The <strong>Manual Tester</strong> workspace view (sidebar → Workspace view) is for functional testers who understand the business process, review requirements and prepare tests, without working with source code, APIs, message queues or infrastructure. It orders the existing modules as a testing workflow; it adds no module and changes no data.</p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Project setup</strong> — <em>Import Project</em> shows where BirkNext gets its information; <em>Source Management</em> shows which version of each source is analysed. When several versions of one source exist, choose the analysis baseline there first: without it, BirkNext draws no implementation-specific conclusion from that source.</li>
        <li><strong>Understand the system</strong> — <em>System Understanding</em>: the business flow, each step, the data, the rules and the gaps, and what is source-backed versus only documented. Its <em>What to test</em> tab shows the test situations the guide describes (preconditions, test data, expected outcome, evidence to observe, each with its state). Select one or more there and choose <em>Prepare in Test Plan Suggestions</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>Requirements</strong> — <em>Specification Explorer</em> (requirements, user stories, acceptance scenarios), <em>Document Quality Review</em> (clarity, completeness and testability of the requirements) and <em>Documentation Drift</em> (documents that no longer match).</li>
        <li><strong>Test preparation</strong> — <em>Test Plan Suggestions</em> is where manual tests are prepared: choose scenarios (grouped by business area), turn them into reviewable Test Plan, Suite and Case drafts, review them, link Test Data, save snapshots, compare deliveries and prepare an Azure DevOps export (see <a href="@UserGuideContent.Href("test-plan-suggestions")">Test Plan Suggestions</a>). <em>Test Data</em> shows which approved test data exists and what is still missing. Formal Test Plans and execution stay in Azure DevOps.</li>
        <li><strong>Coverage &amp; traceability</strong> — <em>Requirements Traceability</em> keeps designed, referenced and executed coverage apart; <em>Test Coverage &amp; Overlap Review</em> finds requirements without tests and overlapping tests.</li>
        <li><strong>Quality &amp; follow-up</strong> — <em>Project Quality</em>, <em>Recommended Workflow</em> and this guide.</li>
    </ol>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>A view is not a permission.</strong> It only decides which modules the sidebar shows and in which order. Modules outside the view are not listed in its sidebar but stay available through the sidebar search (under "Outside current view"), All Modules and their links; Feature Visibility (System Settings) still decides which modules exist, and the backend still decides who may change what. Switching views never changes the project, the analysis baseline or any setting.</li>
        <li><strong>BirkNext and Azure DevOps Test Plans.</strong> BirkNext helps you understand requirements, find functional scenarios, prepare test data and see coverage and change impact. Formal test plans, test suites, Test Case work items, manual execution, test results and defects stay in Azure DevOps Test Plans. BirkNext does not read or write Azure DevOps Test Plans or Test Cases: it prepares reviewable drafts and an export you can use there. A scenario or draft in BirkNext is never an Azure DevOps Test Case and never an executed test.</li>
        <li><strong>Testing without a frontend.</strong> A system without its own screens can still be tested manually, but only when there is an authorised way to give input, controlled test data, a known expected result, an observable outcome (for example a status a tester may look at), a suitable environment and a way to keep evidence. A tester does not need to read API payloads or queues; a technical colleague can provide the input or observation channel.</li>
        <li><strong>XSD validation is not functional testing.</strong> A valid XML message only satisfies the schema's structure and data types. It does not show that a child was identified correctly, that a duplicate or correction was handled, that a reporting period was locked, that the report was stored or that it reached the next system.</li>
        <li><strong>Example (Skolenærvær).</strong> Import the project material, choose the intended M2LB analysis baseline, follow how an attendance report moves from Altinn through MU/MM to the school service in System Understanding, review the requirement for a child that is not yet known, open What to test for its scenarios and their open questions, check the needed test data, review coverage, and then write the formal Test Cases in Azure DevOps. This is an illustration of the workflow, not a statement that any of these tests has been run.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section id="test-plan-suggestions" class="ug-section ug-topic" data-topic="test-plan-suggestions" aria-labelledby="test-plan-suggestions-title">
    <h2 id="test-plan-suggestions-title" class="ug-section-title" tabindex="-1">Test Plan Suggestions</h2>
    <GuideArticleMeta Topic="test-plan-suggestions" />
    <p class="ug-lead"><a href="/test-plan-suggestions">Test Plan Suggestions</a> (Manual Tester → Test preparation) turns scenario candidates from System Understanding → What to test into a reviewable proposal: a Test Plan with Test Suites and Test Case drafts. System Understanding stays the place to understand the system and discover what to test; this page is where you prepare the tests.</p>
    <p data-testid="guide-tps-types"><strong>Test type and execution method:</strong> each case can be classified as functional, integration, end-to-end, performance, security or API / contract, and separately as manual, tool-assisted or automated — a performance case is not automatically automated. For a <strong>performance</strong> case a form appears in plain words: kind of test (load, stress, spike, soak, capacity, baseline), what users do and how many, ramp-up and duration, environment, what to measure and the acceptance criteria. Leave anything unknown empty: it is listed as open (for example <em>Acceptance criteria not defined — review required</em>), never filled in with a guess, and no script is needed. Execution and results stay in Performance Test Review. Type, method and the performance details are kept in snapshots, compared between deliveries and included in the Azure DevOps export draft.</p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Discover</strong> — every scenario of the system guide, with filters by business area, requirement (guide rule), guide state and missing information. Select scenarios here, or use <em>Prepare in Test Plan Suggestions</em> on a scenario (or several selected) in What to test. Selecting never approves anything.</li>
        <li><strong>Suggested Test Plan</strong> — suites are proposed from the guide's business-process stages, never from technical folder names. Move cases, rename suites or add your own.</li>
        <li><strong>Review Test Cases</strong> — the generated content (title, purpose, steps from each step's "how to verify", expected result, requirements) comes from the guide and is kept as it is; your edits are stored separately and marked <em>Edited</em>. Record a decision: under review, needs clarification, excluded (both with a rationale) or accepted for preparation. A case without an expected result cannot be accepted, and an expected result from an unreviewed guide draft is labelled as such. Implementation behaviour is never used as the expected result.</li>
        <li><strong>Test Data &amp; Testability</strong> — link existing Test Data assets by their permanent id (values are never copied) and see, for each case, whether there is an authorised input method, approved test data, a known expected result and a way to observe the outcome. Environment availability and tester permissions are shown as <em>not verified here</em>. A documented scenario is never "ready" by itself. Links to executable journey scenarios or Critical E2E flows are proposed only when the guide names their exact id; a reviewer confirms or rejects them, and nothing is matched by similar titles.</li>
        <li><strong>Saved Snapshots</strong> — <em>Save Test Plan Suggestion Snapshot</em> freezes the whole proposal: scenario revisions, suites, case content, requirements, Test Data revisions, review decisions, testability, the source baseline and the suggestion-rule version. A snapshot can never be changed (the database refuses it); saving identical content again creates nothing.</li>
        <li><strong>Compare Deliveries</strong> — two snapshots, case by case: added, changed, removed and unchanged, with the field that changed. Source, guide, review, Test Data and suggestion-rule differences are kept apart. A changed expected result is called a <em>potential business-rule change</em> only when both sides rest on a reviewed basis; otherwise it needs review. Regression recommendations keep unchanged high-risk and previously blocked cases in view.</li>
        <li><strong>Prepare for Azure DevOps</strong> — copyable Test Case drafts and a structured BirkNext JSON file of one saved snapshot, with the unresolved questions. Nothing is written to Azure DevOps, and import compatibility with Azure DevOps is not claimed.</li>
    </ol>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>When the guide changes.</strong> A case whose scenario changed in the guide is flagged. Nothing is rewritten until you choose <em>Create new proposal revision from the current guide</em>; an accepted case whose scenario changed then goes back to review.</li>
        <li><strong>Source baseline.</strong> The proposal records which source snapshot each guide source uses. When a source is imported in alternative versions without a selected analysis baseline, the page says so and the versions are never combined.</li>
        <li><strong>Permissions.</strong> Anyone with the local session can read proposals. Creating, editing, reviewing and saving snapshots need the configuration-write permission, the same as editing the system guide.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section id="recommended-workflow" class="ug-section ug-topic" data-topic="recommended-workflow" aria-labelledby="recommended-workflow-title">
    <h2 id="recommended-workflow-title" class="ug-section-title" tabindex="-1">Recommended Workflow</h2>
    <GuideArticleMeta Topic="recommended-workflow" />
    <p class="ug-lead"><a href="/getting-started">Recommended Workflow</a> recommends the most useful next step with the evidence and configuration available now. It is guidance, not a mandatory sequence or a release decision.</p>
    <p>When several things are possible it offers <strong>review paths</strong> instead of one forced step: <strong>Source review</strong> (ready as soon as source is analyzed — infrastructure-only packages included), <strong>Document review</strong> (needs supported project documents) and <strong>Runtime / integration review</strong>. A missing input blocks only the work that needs it: missing documents block document reviews, a missing Target Environment blocks runtime reviews, and browser authentication matters only for runtime runs that need it (unattended login only for automated execution).</p>
    <p>On the runtime path the setup order is: <strong>Target Environment</strong> (where you test) → <strong>Target Application</strong> (what is deployed there) → <strong>Project evidence</strong> (which imported packages describe it) → set the environment active → run. Configuring an application or Project never activates an environment; Recommended Workflow asks you to set it active only when you are about to run runtime work.</p>
    <div class="ug-two-col">
        <div>
            <h3 class="ug-subtitle">Typical next actions</h3>
            <ul class="ug-list">
                <li><strong>Choose what you want to review</strong> — no project documents to review yet, so source, document and runtime paths are offered side by side.</li>
                <li><strong>Configure project context</strong> — an active environment has imported evidence but no Project yet.</li>
                <li><strong>Use the imported project documentation</strong> — documents exist in an imported package but are not associated with the Target Application yet (configure it, or review an uncertain source).</li>
                <li><strong>Import project documents</strong> — the Project requires documentation that no package holds.</li>
                <li><strong>Choose a module / Needs selection</strong> — several candidates and none selected.</li>
                <li><strong>Analyze the source again</strong> — source evidence needs refresh.</li>
                <li>The next review step — explorers, traceability, integration or testing reviews.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
            <h3 class="ug-subtitle">What it never does</h3>
            <ul class="ug-list">
                <li>Ask you to <em>Load project artifacts</em> when imported evidence already exists — that step appears only when nothing is imported.</li>
                <li>Recommend an Unavailable feature.</li>
                <li>Treat input completeness as a quality score.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
    <h3 id="review-decisions" class="ug-subtitle">Review decisions</h3>
    <p>Each review step says where it stands: <em>Ready to review</em>, <em>Reviewed</em>, <em>Reviewed with gaps</em> (completed, not a failure), <em>Approved</em> (only where a policy asks), <em>Review stale</em> (the artifact changed), <em>Blocked</em> or <em>Not applicable</em>. A decision names the exact file and content fingerprint it applies to; opening an Explorer records nothing. When the document changes, the earlier decision stays in history but no longer counts. <em>Review decisions</em> counts required steps with a recorded decision; optional steps are shown but not counted.</p>
</section>
